<article id="post-21342" class="post-21342 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-metro tag-councilmember-courtney-johnston tag-councilmember-rollin-horton tag-councilmember-sean-parker tag-councilmember-tasha-ellis tag-kyonzte-toombs-2 tag-metro-council tag-nashville tag-nashville-metro-council-meeting tag-the-nations tag-vice-mayor-angie-henderson tag-voice-of-the-nations tag-woody-baum tag-zoning entry">
	<div class="entry-content">

		
		<aside>			<div
								class="newspack-popup-container newspack-popup hidden   newspack-inline-popup       "
				role="button"
				tabindex="0"
				style="background-color:#EEEEEE;color:#000"
				id="id_20166"
				data-segments=""
				data-frequency="0,0,0,month"
											>
				
<div class="wp-block-group alignfull" style="padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-efdcd2e1 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="has-light-gray-background-color has-background wp-block-paragraph"><em>🦅 </em><strong>The best of the <em>Banner</em> </strong>in your inbox with The Flyer newsletter.</p>


	<div
		class="wp-block-newspack-newsletters-subscribe newspack-newsletters-subscribe is-style-modern"
		data-success-message="Thank you for signing up!"
					>
					<form id="newspack-subscribe-1" data-newspack-recaptcha="newspack_newsletter_signup">
				<input type="hidden" name="newspack_newsletters_subscribe" value="1" />
							<input
				name="newspack_popup_id"
				type="hidden"
				value="20166"
			/>
			<input
				name="prompt_title"
				type="hidden"
				value="Newsletter Above Article"
			/>
											<input type="hidden" name="lists[]" value="ae2961c671" />
																<div class="newspack-newsletters-email-input">
										<input
						id="newspack-newsletters-subscribe-block-input-37055-email"
						type="email"
						name="npe"
						autocomplete="email"
						placeholder="Email Address"
						value=""
					/>
						<input class="nphp" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" name="email" type="email" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Email Address" />
						
					<button class="submit-button"type="submit" style="">
						<span class="submit">Sign up</span>
					</button>
				</div>
			</form>
				<div class="newspack-newsletters-subscribe__response">
			<div class="newspack-newsletters-subscribe__icon"></div>
			<div class="newspack-newsletters-subscribe__message">
							</div>
		</div>
	</div>
	</div></div>
			</div>
		</aside>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After weeks of debate, the Metro Council voted for a third and final time to approve a <a href="https://www.nashvillepost.com/business/development/rezoning-plan-proposed-for-the-nations/article_992c3b57-a897-4556-8fee-3dc3f143ed1a.html">rezoning and neighborhood plan for The Nations</a>.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Led by Councilmember Rollin Horton, the plan allows for increased housing density in The Nations while adding new stormwater, tree protection and design requirements. It has triggered debate both within the neighborhood and among the city’s pro-housing and anti-development factions.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The discussion among councilmembers devolved rapidly. After Councilmember Tasha Ellis interrupted Councilmember Sean Parker when he was speaking in support of the bill, Parker walked back to Ellis’ desk. Ellis said, “This fella came to my desk threatening me.” Ellis later told the <em>Banner</em> that Parker asked her, “What the fuck are you doing?”</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When Vice Mayor Angie Henderson sought to reassert authority over the meeting, Ellis said, “You don’t tell me what I should do. I’m tired of you sitting up there using your white privilege. … You’re not my momma.”</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Henderson called for a five-minute recess that stretched into 15. Most of the members of the Minority Caucus retreated with Ellis to a committee room off the floor, where they met and would not allow the <em>Banner</em> to remain. The <em>Banner</em> cited the Tennessee Open Meetings Act to the members.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back on the floor, Kyonzté Toombs, chair of the Minority Caucus, called Parker’s use of profanity and manner of speaking to Ellis an “act of intimidation and it can’t be tolerated.” Specifically, Toombs cited Parker’s status as a white man and Ellis’ as a seated Black woman. The caucus demanded an apology from Parker, who offered a brief one. </p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Efforts to defer final consideration for further refinement were not successful. Horton said he has been discussing the plan in public meetings for nearly two years, and he previously deferred council consideration of the legislation earlier this year.&nbsp;</p>
<aside class="scaip scaip-1    "><style>@media ( min-width: 300px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-1{min-height: 100px;}}@media ( min-width: 320px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-1{min-height: 100px;}}@media ( min-width: 728px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-1{min-height: 90px;}}</style>		<div class='newspack_global_ad scaip-1 fixed-height'>
						<!-- /23007974146/newspack-in-article-1-660aee8482f6c --><div id='div-gpt-ad-8abf920c63-0'></div>		</div>
		</aside>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Opponents expressed concerns about traffic and crowded streets, as well as stressed the need for infrastructure improvements and a changing neighborhood character. Supporters cited a need for more housing and a hope that the new zoning will create improved walkability, generate new tax revenue and provide for different housing styles that are harder to build under existing zoning.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Woody Baum, a Nations resident and board member of the neighborhood association that backed the plan, called it “an objective improvement over the status quo.”</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Councilmember Courtney Johnston asked Horton to delay final consideration to “show good faith and goodwill” to constituents who missed out on the months of meetings and were coming to the debate more recently. She said Horton “obviously put in a lot of work.”</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A group called Voice of the Nations, which was formed in recent weeks, sent out a press release prior to Tuesday’s meeting. Members of the group have been vocal in their objections to the legislation, but the group stated in the release that it agrees with Horton on several parts of the plan, including allowing four units on a lot in certain circumstances. That group, too, requested a deferral to further refine the plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>

<h3 class="wp-block-heading">New leadership</h3>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This week marked the final committee meetings for most of the Metro Council’s committee chairs. Vice Mayor Angie Henderson last week announced appointments for the new legislative year, effective at the first meeting in September. Kyonzté Toombs is taking over leadership of the Budget Committee from Delishia Porterfield. (See the rest of the new appointments <a href="https://nashvillebanner.com/2025/08/18/heat-advisory-middle-tennessee-3/">here</a>.)&nbsp;</p>
<aside class="scaip scaip-2    "><style>@media ( min-width: 300px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-2{min-height: 100px;}}@media ( min-width: 320px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-2{min-height: 100px;}}@media ( min-width: 728px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-2{min-height: 90px;}}</style>		<div class='newspack_global_ad scaip-2 fixed-height'>
						<!-- /23007974146/newspack-in-article-2-660aee8522bda --><div id='div-gpt-ad-a15bd4e449-0'></div>		</div>
		</aside>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The council also took nominations for some elected leadership roles. Sandra Sepulveda’s one-year term as president pro tempore is up, and Porterfield and Russ Bradford are running to fill the position. The president pro tem oversees the council in the vice mayor’s absence.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The chair of the Planning &amp; Zoning Committee is elected to a two-year term and also serves on the Metro Planning Commission. <a href="https://nashvillebanner.com/2025/07/29/john-rutherford-rollin-horton-planning-committee/">John Rutherford and Rollin Horton are running for the position</a>. In perhaps a preview of the race, Rutherford moved for a two-meeting deferral of Horton’s Nations legislation during Monday’s committee meeting. Rutherford said he is “not opposed to” Horton’s legislation, but said it’s important to “get it right” if Horton’s push is going to serve as a model for other parts of town.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The election for those positions (plus representatives to the Traffic and Parking Commission, Metro Audit Committee and Short Term Rental Appeals Board) will be held at the Sept. 2 meeting.&nbsp;</p>

<h3 class="wp-block-heading">OHS contract</h3>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Councilmembers pressed Office of Homeless Services and Metro Finance officials for answers as they considered a <a href="https://nashville.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=7512689&amp;GUID=DF93A2EA-943B-4E0B-99D2-182A21F6E7F7">resolution</a> approving a $3.5 million management contract for Strobel House, the city’s first public permanent supportive housing facility. The office has been paying contractor Depaul USA for months without a contract in place, an oversight highlighted by blogger (and former Metro employee) <a href="https://substack.com/home/post/p-171344469">Mike Lacy</a>.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">OHS Director April Calvin acknowledged the situation was “not an ideal process” and highlighted a series of “corrective actions” made by the department. According to Calvin, the office first learned it was not in compliance with procurement policies about three months ago. OHS leaders are participating in a state training program for local finance officers and working with Metro Finance on further changes.&nbsp;</p>
<aside class="scaip scaip-3    "><style>@media ( min-width: 300px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-3{min-height: 100px;}}@media ( min-width: 320px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-3{min-height: 100px;}}@media ( min-width: 728px ){.newspack_global_ad.scaip-3{min-height: 90px;}}</style>		<div class='newspack_global_ad scaip-3 fixed-height'>
						<!-- /23007974146/newspack-in-article-3-660aee8582916 --><div id='div-gpt-ad-60b5adeb34-0'></div>		</div>
		</aside>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“It looks really terrible for Metro,” Councilmember Zulfat Suara said in Tuesday’s Public Health &amp; Safety Committee meeting. “I was shocked.”</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The council debated deferring the resolution before passing it.&nbsp;</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Mayor Freddie O’Connell and OHS leaders are set to celebrate the one-year anniversary of Strobel House later this week.</p>
